# Install with: sudo install -m 440 -o root -g root sudoers.d/mediaclipmakarr-deploy /etc/sudoers.d/mediaclipmakarr-deploy
# Always validate with `visudo -cf` before installing — a syntax error in
# /etc/sudoers.d/ can lock out sudo entirely.
#
# This grants mediaclipmakarr-deploy exactly one capability: running
# /opt/deploy/mediaclipmakarr-deploy-executor.sh as root, with any
# arguments, no password prompt. That's intentionally broad at the sudoers
# level — branch names are dynamic, and trying to enumerate every exact
# (branch, env-file) combination here would either need updating on every
# new branch or would have to be wide enough to be meaningless. The actual
# enforcement lives inside that script: it independently validates both
# arguments against its own hardcoded allowlist before doing anything, and
# never passes them through a shell (no eval, no bash -c, no string-built
# commands) — so there's nothing for this wildcard to let through unsafely.
# This is the standard, documented sudoers pattern for a command that does
# its own argument validation.
#
# This account should NOT also be a member of the `docker` group — that
# would make this rule pointless, since group membership alone already
# grants full daemon control.

# SSH exec_command sessions (what the MCP server uses) have no controlling
# tty. Some distros default to `Defaults requiretty`, which would make sudo
# refuse to run at all in that context — this line overrides that for just
# this one account.
Defaults:mediaclipmakarr-deploy !requiretty

mediaclipmakarr-deploy ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/deploy/mediaclipmakarr-deploy-executor.sh *
